{
  "source": "What the 4FGL reader must read and refuse, as rows of a table shaped like 4FGL's LAT_Point_Source_Catalog (column names and formats as the 4FGL FITS file has them, but for Source_Name, which is 20A here and 18A there: fitsio writes a string with a NUL in front, which would cut the 18th character of a name, and the last character is what tells an extended source) that fermi_test.go writes in memory. Positions, ellipses and fluxes are float32 in the file, so they are compared to a part in a million (rel), which is far wider than float32's 6e-8 and far finer than any Fermi ellipse. The ellipse columns are in degrees at 95%: 0.05 deg is 180 arcsec and 0.025 deg is 90, and one sigma is the stated radius over sqrt(2 ln 20) = 2.4477468306808166: 180 gives 73.53701687765429, 90 gives 36.76850843882714, 144 gives 58.82961350212343 and 72 gives 29.414806751061715. A row whose second axis is the longer swaps them, and its angle turns a quarter turn with them: 0.02 and 0.04 deg at 30 deg is 144 and 72 arcsec at 120. A negative angle is brought into 0 to 360: -10 is 350. Flags is 4FGL's analysis flags: nonzero is doubtful, zero is good; the kept flags are Flags, CLASS1 and ASSOC1 in that order, with the file's blank padding trimmed. A refused row names its column, and says which kind of source has no ellipse: names ending in e are extended, and the rest are point sources whose position 4FGL fixes from outside (a nova, a pulsar, the Crab's components). A refused row is followed by a good one in the file, and the reader goes on to it. No test here reads the real 4FGL file (gll_psc_v35.fit, 7.8 MB, not committed): a test that skipped when it was absent would pass without checking anything. Once, on 2026-10-29, the reader was run by hand on it: 81 rows refused as extended and 9 as point sources with a fixed position.",
